   (b) include, embed, bundle, link (statically or dynamically), load, invoke
       as a sub-process or sidecar, call via IPC, RPC, or network, expose
       through a plug-in interface, or otherwise combine the Software so that
       it supplies material functionality to a Commercial Product — process,
       container, package, or network separation does not avoid this
       restriction, and it makes no difference whether You, an intermediary,
       or the end user performs the final combination, loading, or linking;
   (c) Provide the Software as a Service as, or as part of, a Commercial
       Product, including charging a Fee for hosting of, access to, support
       for, or any service whose value derives materially from the Software's
       functionality, and including operating the Software for or on behalf
       of third parties; or
   (d) distribute the Software together with, as a dependency of, as a
       recommended or auto-installed component of, or designed or marketed to
       be combined with a Commercial Product, even if the Software is
       nominally free, separately packaged, or separately licensed (no "mere
       aggregation" or "the user combines it" workaround).
   These restrictions reach making the SOFTWARE'S OWN functionality the thing
   a third party consumes for a Fee. They do NOT restrict selling products or
   services that You merely built, tested, secured, or audited with the
   Software, where the Software's own functionality is not itself made
   available to Your customers as the offering or as a feature of it; that
   case is the permitted Internal Use described in Section 3. You may not use
   affiliates, subsidiaries, contractors, special-purpose entities, or staged
   redistribution to accomplish indirectly what this Section prohibits. To
   the extent Section 3 and this Section 4 conflict, this Section 4 controls.
